#--------------------------------------------------------------------

#
# name: symbol that looks like a boolean keyword
#====================================================================


# design decisions for Section 4 (double-quoted strings):
#
#   Double-quoted strings carry a `dq: true` flag on the value atom. CaspJ
#   stores the source bytes between the delimiting quotes verbatim — the
#   #{expr} interp syntax, backslash escapes, and everything else are
#   preserved as literal chars in the value. The runtime String class reads
#   the dq flag and does interpolation + escape processing at that point.
#   CaspJ sees a dq string as a normal string plus one flag; runtime handles
#   the rest.
#
#   Rationale: keeps CaspJ minimal (no interp-shape sub-tree, no escape
#   sub-decoding at parse time), and puts interpolation where variable
#   bindings live — the runtime.
#
#   Single-quoted strings keep their existing shape `{value: "..."}` with no
#   flag; they're literal-only in every consumer.

