#
# name: object-as-method as RHS of assignment
#====================================================================


# design decisions for Section 29 (objects as methods):
#
#   `$recv.$callable_var args` invokes the callable stored in $callable_var
#   with $recv as the receiver. The callable is any object that responds to
#   .call — typically a function, closure, or singleton method captured into
#   a variable.
#
#   CaspianJ shape: same slot as a named method call, but position [2] is a
#   value-atom (the callable expression) instead of a bareword string.
#     [recv, {var: "myfunc"}, {args: [...]}]     — with args
#     [recv, {var: "myfunc"}]                    — no args
#
#   Runtime dispatch rule: if position [2] is a string, do named-method
#   dispatch. If position [2] is a table (value-atom), evaluate it, expect
#   the result to be callable, and invoke it with recv as the receiver.
#
#   Deferred to the %fetch section: `$recv.%('url').name` and
#   `$recv.%(url)` — the callable-in-method-position rule extends to any
#   value-atom, but the %(url) short form isn't parsed yet.
#
#   Deferred: methods held in $$varobj, in @field lookups, in %sys.attr
#   chains, or produced by arbitrary expressions. Add tests as the
#   callable-expression surface widens.

